#!/bin/sh
# pre-rebase — refuse to rewrite history that the evidence ledger names.
#
# WHAT THIS PREVENTS. Every row the daemon freezes carries the revision of the
# build that produced it (store.CodeRevision, written onto prediction_ledger and
# regime_outcomes). tools/accuracy_registry.py then refuses any verdict whose
# contributing rows name a commit this repository cannot resolve — and that
# refusal is all-or-nothing and permanent, because the gate's window is anchored
# to a fixed epoch, so an offending row never ages out.
#
# A rebase is therefore a one-way door. Rewriting a commit that the ledger
# references does not "move" it: the old object becomes unreachable, every row
# naming it turns into evidence nobody can attribute, and the predictor those
# rows feed loses its verdict for good. That is not a hypothetical. On
# 2026-08-02 three CLEAN builds — 3020f058, 3b80a09c, 3c096b33 — wrote 946 rows
# and were then rewritten out of the repository. Nothing noticed until an audit
# read the gate two days later, and recovering from it took a chained
# pre-registration amendment (kind "revision-epoch-correction").
#
# The daemon's own startup gate (lineage.BuildReachable) catches this too, but
# only at the NEXT RESTART, which can be days. This hook catches it at the
# moment of the rewrite, which is the only moment it is still cheap to undo.
#
# POSTURE. Refuse only on a PROVEN collision: a referenced commit that exists
# now and sits inside the range about to be rewritten. When the question cannot
# be answered — no database, no sqlite, no rev-list — the hook allows the
# rebase, because an unprovable answer must never be read as a refusal. That is
# the same rule revisionResolvableCached and BuildReachable follow.
#
# Only CLEAN stamps are protected. A "+dirty" or unstamped row is already
# unattributable, so rewriting the commit underneath it changes nothing.
#
# INSTALL:  git config core.hooksPath ops/githooks
# OVERRIDE: SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE=1 git rebase ...
set -u

upstream="${1:-}"
branch="${2:-}"
[ -n "$upstream" ] || exit 0

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || exit 0
cd "$root" || exit 0

if [ -n "${SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE:-}" ]; then
  echo "pre-rebase: SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE set — ledger check skipped." >&2
  exit 0
fi

db="${SIGNALDECK_DB:-data/signaldeck.db}"
if [ ! -f "$db" ]; then
  exit 0 # No ledger here, so no evidence to strand.
fi

# shellcheck source=/dev/null
. "$root/ops/lib-portable.sh" 2>/dev/null || exit 0

# The commits this rebase would rewrite. Empty range or a rev-list failure both
# mean nothing provable is at stake.
rewrite="$(git rev-list "$upstream..${branch:-HEAD}" 2>/dev/null)" || exit 0
[ -n "$rewrite" ] || exit 0

refs="$(sd_sqlite_read "$db" "
  SELECT DISTINCT revision FROM prediction_ledger WHERE revision IS NOT NULL
  UNION
  SELECT DISTINCT revision FROM regime_outcomes  WHERE revision IS NOT NULL;" 2>/dev/null)" || {
  echo "pre-rebase: WARNING — could not read $db; ledger-reference check SKIPPED." >&2
  exit 0
}
[ -n "$refs" ] || exit 0

doomed="$(mktemp)" || exit 0
rangefile="$(mktemp)" || { rm -f "$doomed"; exit 0; }
trap 'rm -f "$doomed" "$rangefile"' EXIT INT TERM
printf '%s\n' "$rewrite" >"$rangefile"

printf '%s\n' "$refs" | while IFS= read -r rev; do
  # Already unattributable, or not a commit id at all: nothing to protect.
  case "$rev" in
    *+dirty | "") continue ;;
  esac
  [ "${#rev}" -eq 40 ] || continue
  # Only a commit that still resolves can be LOST by this rebase.
  git cat-file -e "$rev^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || continue
  grep -qxF "$rev" "$rangefile" || continue
  printf '%s\n' "$rev" >>"$doomed"
done

[ -s "$doomed" ] || exit 0

echo "" >&2
echo "REFUSING TO REBASE: this rewrites commit(s) the evidence ledger references." >&2
echo "" >&2
while IFS= read -r rev; do
  rows="$(sd_sqlite_read "$db" "
    SELECT COUNT(*) || ' ledger row(s), ' ||
           MIN(date(predicted_at,'unixepoch')) || ' .. ' ||
           MAX(date(predicted_at,'unixepoch'))
      FROM prediction_ledger WHERE revision = '$rev';" 2>/dev/null)"
  printf '  %s  %s\n' "$rev" "${rows:-(row count unavailable)}" >&2
done <"$doomed"
cat >&2 <<'EOF'

Rewriting these orphans every row above: the grader can no longer resolve the
build that produced them, so it strips the verdict from the whole predictor
family — permanently, because those rows never leave its window.

Options, in order of preference:
  1. Rebase a range that stops short of these commits.
  2. Leave them in history and put your change on top instead.
  3. If you are certain the evidence is disposable:
       SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE=1 git rebase ...
     Then file a chained correction, as the 2026-08-02 incident required:
       go run -C daemon ./cmd/prereg-amend -kind revision-epoch-correction
EOF
exit 1
